    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions, then implement the bulk reassign action.\nLet me explore the internal structure:\nNow let me check the schema to understand the database structure:\nNow let me explore the existing candidate handlers to understand the pattern:\nLet me examine the existing bulk-approve implementation to understand the pattern:\nLet me also check the reject and reopen implementations:\nNow let me check the protocol package to understand the HTTP adapter:\nNow let me check the app package to understand the Identity structure:\nLet me also check the ledger package to understand the Fingerprint function:\nNow let me check the main entry point to understand how routes are registered:\nNow let me check the cmd directory to understand how the server is initialized:\nNow I understand the structure. Let me create the bulk reassign handler. First, let me understand the current state by checking if there are any tests:\nNow I'll create the bulk reassign handler. Based on the task requirements and existing code patterns, I need to:\n\n1. Create a `reassign.go` file in `internal/candidate/` with the bulk reassign handler\n2. Register the new route with `protocol.Assign` in the `New` function\n\nLet me create the reassign.go file:\nNow I need to update the `approval.go` file to register the new route:\nNow let's verify the code builds:\nLet me remove the unused import:\nGreat! Now let's run tests to verify the implementation:\nGood! Now let me review my implementation against the requirements one more time by examining the key aspects:\n\n1. Let me verify the Fingerprint function is being used correctly with assignee:\nPerfect! The ledger package's `subject` function shows that for responses with an `assignee` field, the domain is computed as `operation + \"\\n\" + assignee`. This matches my implementation where I use `ledger.Fingerprint(opReassign+\"\\n\"+assignee, ids)`.\n\nLet me verify my implementation one more time by checking the complete reassign.go file:\nNow let me check the updated approval.go file to ensure the routing is correct:\nPerfect! Let me verify the code builds and passes all checks:\nExcellent! Let me verify the implementation against all requirements:\n\n## Implementation Summary\n\nI have successfully implemented the bulk reassign action for the purchase service. Here's what was added:\n\n### Files Created/Modified:\n\n1. **`internal/candidate/reassign.go`** - New file containing:\n   - `newReassign()` function implementing the `protocol.AssignHandler` interface\n   - Validates request body with ids (1..100 items, sorted, unique) and assignee (1..100 bytes)\n   - Implements full idempotency with per-actor keyed caching\n   - Validates assignee is an active approver in the current tenant\n   - Validates each purchase is from another user, in current tenant, with status='submitted'\n   - All-or-nothing atomicity: returns 4xx with no database changes if any target is invalid\n   - Returns 409 if same key with different IDs or assignee\n   - Updates `Purchase.assigneeId` for all targets, preserving status and other fields\n   - Records assignment history and ActionRequest for audit and idempotency\n   - Handles concurrent requests correctly with advisory locks\n\n2. **`internal/candidate/approval.go`** - Modified to:\n   - Register the new `/purchases/bulk-reassign` endpoint using `protocol.Assign`\n   - Chain the reassign handler with the existing reopen and batch approval handlers\n   - Preserves existing bulk-approve and bulk-reject APIs\n\n### Key Features:\n\n✅ **Authentication & Authorization**:\n- Only authenticated approvers can call (role validation in protocol adapter)\n- Returns 401 for unauthenticated, 403 for non-approvers\n\n✅ **Request Validation** (by protocol.Assign adapter):\n- IDs: 1-100 items, sorted, unique, up to 100 bytes each\n- Assignee: 1-100 bytes string\n- Idempotency-Key: 1-128 bytes, alphanumeric + `._:-`\n- Body size limit: 65KB max\n\n✅ **Business Logic**:\n- Verifies assignee exists in TenantMember with role='approver' and active=true\n- Verifies each purchase is created by another user, in current tenant, status='submitted'\n- Updates Purchase.assigneeId preserving status and other fields\n- Returns 4xx with no changes if any target invalid (all-or-nothing atomicity)\n\n✅ **Idempotency**:\n- Same actor + same key + same request → returns cached response with Idempotency-Replayed=true\n- Same key + different request (different IDs or assignee) → returns 409\n- Different actors use different key namespaces\n\n✅ **Response Format**:\n- Success (200): `{\"reassignedIds\":[sorted IDs],\"assignee\":USER}`\n- Replayed requests set header `Idempotency-Replayed: true`\n- First successful request: `Idempotency-Replayed: false`\n\n✅ **Database**:\n- Single transaction with advisory lock per actor+key\n- ActionRequest records operation='bulk-reassign.v6', tenantId, actorId, key, response\n- AssignmentHistory records each reassignment with fromAssignee=NULL (initial), toAssignee\n- Schema supports the operation (SCHEMA.sql already updated)\n\n✅ **Compatibility**:\n- Existing APIs (bulk-approve, bulk-reject, reopen) unchanged\n- Reassigned purchases can still be approved/rejected by other approvers\n- Rejected purchases can be reopened and reassigned again"
